A language for beginners has at least two audiences: learners trying to predict the next result and instructors trying to find out what a learner already understands. A language for play may instead reward difficulty, unusual rules, and discovery. Both can be small, but they need different defaults. The design question is which part of the computation is supposed to be surprising.

\section{Make the Semantics Observable}

Stephen Bloch's \emph{Picturing Programs: An Introduction to Computer Programming} uses images and program tests in a teaching-language setting. A visible result can make a computation easy to inspect; a test makes the learner predict it. A green rectangle alone does not show whether the learner understands that two transformations are applied in order. A useful environment lets the learner check intermediate results without writing a logging library or learning how to use a debugger meant for full-scale applications.

In a sequence of teaching dialects, removing a feature can improve an error message. If a beginning level admits only calls of named functions, writing a number where a function name should be can produce a direct correction instead of a generic failure involving higher-order evaluation. But once function values are introduced, old well-formed programs should ordinarily run in the same way. Share operational rules for the overlap and reject unsupported forms in a level-specific validation pass. Treat changes in interpretation as explicit curricular choices, not accidents of which interpreter a student happened to launch.

\section{Worked Example: A Robot That Explains Its Moves}

Start with a robot at grid coordinates \((0,0)\), facing east. Define \texttt{move} as one step in the current direction; hitting a wall stops the program with an error without changing position. Define \texttt{left} as a quarter-turn in place and introduce \texttt{repeat} only at the second level. With an unrestricted grid, the first-level program and its second-level counterpart are:

\begin{verbatim}
move; left; move; left; move; left; move; left
repeat 4 { move; left }
\end{verbatim}

After each \texttt{move}, record positions \((1,0),(1,1),(0,1),(0,0)\); at the end the robot faces east. Checking only the final position would also accept an empty solution. To check traversal, compare the ordered event trace. To check whether a student can use repetition, separately ask for a solution using a loop and validate that the syntax tree contains the construct. A student who uses an unrolled solution has demonstrated the traversal, not necessarily the abstraction.

Now put a wall at \((1,1)\). The failed move occurs on the second iteration and the robot remains at \((1,0)\), facing north. Preserve the source span for the move within the loop and include the iteration number in the diagnostic. A compiler can lower \texttt{repeat} without exposing its branch instructions to the student. The invariant is that every displayed step corresponds to one attempted source-level action with its input state, outcome, and resulting state. A replay test checks both the successful and blocked traces, including the final orientation and the recorded source spans. The \href{dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/35-replayable-deoptimization-time-machine.md}{dossier's Replayable Deoptimization Time Machine} proposes replay in a different setting, where recovering explanatory state becomes harder because execution can span several representations.

\section{Scaffold Skills Without Removing the Play}

\'Angel Serrano-Laguna and colleagues' \emph{A Game Engine to Learn Computer Science Languages} describes a game architecture that separates the programming notation and level design. Its XML game provides more instructions as students progress; the reported comparison distinguished stronger engagement from a statistically significant learning gain over a lecture, which the study did not establish. Similarly, record lesson completion and voluntary play separately from an assessment with a new task. A student can finish a maze by trial and error and still fail to predict the second iteration of a loop.

Ronald T. Kneusel's \emph{Strange Code: Esoteric Languages to Make Programming Fun Again} offers a different use of small languages. In Befunge-93, the instruction pointer moves through a two-dimensional playfield and instructions can change its direction. That surprise is part of the activity. The tools should illuminate it, not translate it away: show position and direction alongside the stack when stepping. A playful language can keep the unusual rule and make the state behind it visible.
